<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="AZD: FAQ and troubleshooting" id="Azd-FAQ">

    <primary-label ref="azd"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <link-summary>Answers to common AZD questions and fixes for sign-in, proxy, credential storage, display and language server problems.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Fix sign-in, proxy, credential, display and language server problems in AZD.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Troubleshoot the AZD Azure DevOps plugin for JetBrains IDEs: sign-in and token errors, proxies,
        credential storage, blank views, YAML completion and licensing.</web-summary>

    <chapter title="The AZD tool windows are missing" id="tool-windows-missing">
        <p>
            The <control>AZD Pull Requests</control>, <control>AZD Pipelines</control> and <control>AZD Boards</control> tool windows
            appear only when the project has a Git remote that points to Azure DevOps, such as
            <code>https://dev.azure.com/demo-org/demo-project/_git/demo-project</code>.
            Check the remotes in <ui-path>Git | Manage Remotes</ui-path>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Sign-in fails" id="login_pat_issues">
        <list type="bullet">
            <li>
                <p><b>Expired token:</b> check the expiry date of the personal access token in Azure DevOps, and create a new one if needed.
                    In the tool window, select <control>Change Repository or Account…</control> and then <control>Renew Token</control>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><b>Missing scopes or organizations:</b> the token must have the
                    <a href="Azd-Installation.topic" anchor="generate_pat">required scopes</a> and be created for
                    <control>All accessible organizations</control>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><b>Organization in the URL:</b> enter the server URL in <control>Server</control> and the organization or collection
                    in <control>Organization/Collection</control>. AZD rejects a URL that contains the organization or collection.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><b>Azure DevOps Server:</b> Microsoft and Azure CLI sign-in work only with Azure DevOps Services. Use a personal access token.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><b>Azure CLI:</b> if AZD reports that <code>az</code> was not found, install the Azure CLI or set its path in
                    <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Preferences</ui-path>.
                    If it reports that you are not signed in, run <code>az login</code> in a terminal and try again.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><b>Sign-in error in a tool window:</b> click <control>Log in again…</control> in the error message.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><b>Account missing on another computer:</b> AZD accounts are not synchronized by Backup and Sync. Sign in on each computer.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><b>Git asks for credentials:</b> if the Git credential helper is enabled, Git operations do not use your AZD accounts.
                    Sign in through the credential helper, or turn it off in your Git configuration.</p>
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="How do I create a personal access token?" id="create_pat">
        <procedure title="Create a PAT in Azure DevOps" id="create-pat-procedure">
            <step><p>Sign in to Azure DevOps in the browser.</p></step>
            <step><p>Open <ui-path>User settings | Personal access tokens</ui-path> and click <control>New Token</control>.</p></step>
            <step><p>Select <control>All accessible organizations</control> and the scopes listed in
                <a href="Azd-Installation.topic" anchor="generate_pat">Create a personal access token</a>.</p></step>
            <step><p>Click <control>Create</control> and copy the token.</p></step>
        </procedure>
        <note>Treat the token like a password. Do not share it or commit it to source control.</note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Marking a file as viewed fails" id="mark_file_viewed_401">
        <p>
            When AZD cannot mark a pull request file as viewed, it shows <control>File viewed sync requires full access</control>.
            Azure DevOps allows this only with full access.
        </p>
        <list type="bullet">
            <li><p><b>Sign in with Microsoft:</b> remove the account, then sign in again and select <control>Full access</control>.</p></li>
            <li><p><b>Personal access token:</b> create a token with <control>Full access</control> and renew the token in AZD.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Credentials are not saved" id="token_issue">
        <p>
            AZD keeps credentials in the IDE password storage.
            If your system blocks the native keychain, the IDE may keep passwords in memory only, and you must sign in after every restart.
            The AZD accounts page shows a warning in that case.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Store passwords in KeePass" id="fix-password-settings">
            <step><p>Open <ui-path>Settings | Appearance &amp; Behavior | System Settings | Passwords</ui-path>.</p></step>
            <step><p>Select <control>In KeePass</control> and click <control>OK</control>.</p></step>
            <step><p>Sign in to Azure DevOps again.</p></step>
        </procedure>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Requests fail behind a corporate proxy" id="proxy-tls">
        <list type="bullet">
            <li><p>To send AZD requests through the IDE proxy, select <control>Respect proxy configuration</control> in
                <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Preferences</ui-path>.</p></li>
            <li><p>If the proxy inspects TLS traffic and AZD reports certificate errors, select <control>Accept untrusted SSL certificates</control>
                on the same page. It affects only AZD connections.</p></li>
            <li><p>When a proxy interrupts a request, AZD retries it and keeps you signed in.
                It does not ask you for a new token in that case.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="A view shows a message instead of content" id="jcef-views">
        <p>
            The pipeline diagram, the metrics dashboards and the Kanban board use the IDE built-in browser (JCEF).
            If JCEF is not available, these views show a message instead of staying blank.
            Make sure the IDE runs on the bundled JetBrains Runtime, which includes JCEF.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Pipeline YAML has no completion" id="lsp-nodejs">
        <p>Completion and validation in pipeline files come from the Azure Pipelines language server, which needs Node.js 14 or later.</p>
        <list type="bullet">
            <li><p>Install Node.js, or set its path in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Pipeline Settings</ui-path> and click <control>Validate Node.js</control>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check that <control>Inject Azure DevOps YAML pipeline schema</control> is selected on the same page.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check that the file counts as a pipeline file; see <a href="Azd-Pipeline-Yaml.topic" anchor="pipeline-files">Which files are pipeline files</a>.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The AzureDevOps task server type is missing" id="tasks-missing">
        <p>
            The task server integration needs the Task Management plugin.
            Install it from JetBrains Marketplace; AZD works without it.
            See <a href="Azd-Boards.topic" anchor="tasks-integration">Use work items as IDE tasks</a>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="How do I report a bug or request a feature?" id="azd_bug_feature">
        <procedure title="Report an issue" id="report-issue">
            <step><p>Open the <a href="https://github.com/edgafner/dorkag/issues">DORKAG issue tracker on GitHub</a>.</p></step>
            <step><p>Click <control>New issue</control> and select the matching template.</p></step>
            <step><p>Add the AZD and IDE versions, the steps to reproduce, and log excerpts with tokens removed.</p></step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            If the IDE shows an error from AZD, click <control>Report Exception</control> in the error dialog.
            It opens a prefilled issue on GitHub.
            For more ways to get help, see <a href="Azd-Reference-And-Support.topic"/>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Is AZD free?" id="why_not_free">
        <p>
            AZD is a paid plugin with a 10-day free trial.
            Start the trial or activate a license in <ui-path>Help | Register</ui-path>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Licenses are sold on <a href="https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/22319-azd/pricing">JetBrains Marketplace</a>.
            Reviews on the <a href="https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/22319-azd/reviews">Marketplace page</a> help other users decide.
        </p>
    </chapter>

